    <text>
        <body>
            <div type="edition"><p>mi fleres spulare aritimi fasti rufris trce clen cecha</p></div>
                       
            <div type="translation">
            <p>I am the statue, or votive offering, (which) Fasti Rufris gave according to ritual to
                    Artemis Spulare on behalf of her son.</p></div>

            <div type="commentary">
            <p>Cast of a small Etruscan votive bronze of a standing, naked youth, possibly Apollo
                    (Aplu), housed in the Bibliothèque Nationale in Paris. The figure stands in
                    contrapposto placing his weight on his left leg. He rests his right hand on his
                    hip and there is a short chlamys slung over his forearm. The braceleted left arm
                    of the original is missing from above the elbow here the arm is broken from just
                    below the shoulder. He wears a wreath in his curly hair, a necklace with bullae,
                    and mid-length boots. Of unknown provenience, the statuette has been known since
                    at least the 16th century, when it was in the collection of Alfonso d’Este in
                    the library of the Dukes of Ferrara.</p></div>
            

